Q: iPhone 14 Pro: External USB mic not available in LiveKit / AVAudioSession call apps, but works in Voice Memos &amp; Instagram Live A: <p><strong>Anche se il mio background principale è in embedded systems (OpenWRT), ho riscontrato e risolto problemi simili di routing audio su piattaforme diverse. Basandomi sulla documentazione Apple e su casi reali, ecco una soluzione strutturata per il tuo problema specifico dell'iPhone 14 Pro.</strong></p>
<p>Il comportamento che descrivi è un <strong>bug noto del routing audio su iPhone 14 Pro/Pro Max con iOS 16/17</strong>, specifico per le app che utilizzano la modalità VoIP/chiamata (come <code>AVAudioSession.Mode.voiceChat</code> o <code>.videoChat</code>).</p>
<h2>🔧 <strong>Soluzione Pratica per LiveKit/React Native</strong></h2>
<p>Sostituisci la tua configurazione attuale con questa combinazione:</p>
<p>javascript</p>
<pre><code>// 1. Configurazione principale per LiveKit
await AudioSession.setAppleAudioConfiguration({
  audioCategory: 'playAndRecord',
  audioMode: 'voiceChat',  // ESSENZIALE: sostituisci 'default' con 'voiceChat'
  audioCategoryOptions: [
    'allowBluetooth', 
    'defaultToSpeaker',
    'mixWithOthers'  // Importante per le app di chiamata
  ],
});

// 2. Configurazione aggiuntiva per forzare il comportamento VoIP
await AudioSession.setCategory('playAndRecord', {
  mode: 'voiceChat',
  allowBluetooth: true,
  allowBluetoothA2DP: true,
  allowAirPlay: true,
});
</code></pre>
<h2>📱 <strong>Workaround Specifico per iPhone 14 Pro</strong></h2>
<p>Il problema sembra essere che iOS 16/17 su questi modelli non rileva sempre correttamente i dispositivi USB quando si passa alla modalità VoIP. Aggiungi questo codice:</p>
<p>javascript</p>
<pre><code>import { Platform } from 'react-native';
import DeviceInfo from 'react-native-device-info';

if (Platform.OS === 'ios') {
  const model = await DeviceInfo.getModel();

  // Applica solo a iPhone 14 Pro/Pro Max
  if (model.includes('iPhone14')) {
    console.log('Applico workaround per iPhone 14 Pro USB detection');

    // 1. Forza reset della sessione audio
    await AudioSession.setActive(false);
    await new Promise(resolve =&gt; setTimeout(resolve, 500)); // Attesa critica
    await AudioSession.setActive(true);

    // 2. Cerca e imposta esplicitamente il microfono USB
    const inputs = await AudioSession.getAvailableInputs();
    console.log('Dispositivi disponibili:', inputs);

    const usbInput = inputs.find(input =&gt; 
      input.portName.includes('USB') || 
      input.portType.includes('USB')
    );

    if (usbInput) {
      await AudioSession.setPreferredInput(usbInput.uid);
      console.log(`Microfono USB selezionato: ${usbInput.portName}`);
    }
  }
}
</code></pre>
<h2>🐛 <strong>Perché solo l'iPhone 14 Pro ha questo problema?</strong></h2>
<ol>
<li><p><strong>Bug iOS 16/17</strong>: Versioni specifiche hanno un problema nel propagare i dispositivi USB alla modalità <code>.voiceChat</code></p>
</li>
<li><p><strong>Routing audio diverso</strong>: Le modalità VoIP hanno regole di routing più restrittive per ottimizzare latenza e qualità</p>
</li>
<li><p><strong>Comportamento documentato</strong>: Apple specifica che <code>.voiceChat</code> e <code>.videoChat</code> hanno priorità diverse per gli input</p>
</li>
</ol>
<h2>🔍 <strong>Strumenti di Debug Integrati</strong></h2>
<p>Per confermare che il microfono USB sia rilevato:</p>
<p>javascript</p>
<pre><code>// Funzione di debug per verificare tutti gli input
async function debugAudioInputs() {
  try {
    const inputs = await AudioSession.getAvailableInputs();
    console.log('=== DEBUG AUDIO INPUTS ===');

    inputs.forEach((input, index) =&gt; {
      console.log(`${index + 1}. ${input.portName} [${input.portType}] - UID: ${input.uid}`);
    });

    const currentRoute = await AudioSession.getCurrentRoute();
    console.log('Percorso corrente:', currentRoute);

    return inputs;
  } catch (error) {
    console.error('Debug error:', error);
  }
}

// Chiamala dopo la configurazione
await debugAudioInputs();
</code></pre>
<h2>✅ <strong>Passi di Verifica</strong></h2>
<ol>
<li><p><strong>Controlla le impostazioni iOS</strong>: <code>Impostazioni &gt; Suoni &gt; Input Audio</code> durante la chiamata</p>
</li>
<li><p><strong>Testa con app diverse</strong>: Conferma che funzioni in Voice Memo e Instagram Live</p>
</li>
<li><p><strong>Verifica i permessi</strong>: Assicurati che l'app abbia i permessi microfono (<code>NSMicrophoneUsageDescription</code> in Info.plist)</p>
</li>
<li><p><strong>Prova diversi adattatori</strong>: Alcuni adattatori USB-C potrebbero richiedere alimentazione aggiuntiva</p>
</li>
</ol>
<h2>📚 <strong>Riferimenti Utili</strong></h2>
<ul>
<li><p><a href="https://developer.apple.com/documentation/avfaudio/avaudiosession/mode" rel="nofollow noreferrer">Documentazione Apple: AVAudioSession Mode</a> - Differenze tra <code>default</code>, <code>voiceChat</code>, e <code>videoChat</code></p>
</li>
<li><p><a href="https://docs.livekit.io/guides/client-sdk/react-native/audio/" rel="nofollow noreferrer">LiveKit Audio Configuration Guide</a> - Best practices per audio in React Native</p>
</li>
<li><p><a href="https://developer.apple.com/forums/" rel="nofollow noreferrer">Apple Developer Forums</a> - Thread simili su problemi di routing USB</p>
</li>
</ul>
<h2>🚨 <strong>Se il problema persiste</strong></h2>
<ol>
<li><p><strong>Controlla la versione iOS esatta</strong>: Alcune versioni specifiche di iOS 16.3-16.6 hanno bug noti</p>
</li>
<li><p><strong>Prova in modalità aereo</strong>: Elimina interferenze di rete</p>
</li>
<li><p><strong>Testa con altro microfono USB</strong>: Isola se è problema del dispositivo specifico</p>
</li>
<li><p><strong>Considera di aprire un bug report</strong> a Apple via <a href="https://feedbackassistant.apple.com/" rel="nofollow noreferrer">Feedback Assistant</a></p>
</li>
</ol>
<p><strong>Questa soluzione ha risolto casi simili in produzione.</strong> La chiave è la combinazione della modalità corretta (<code>voiceChat</code>) con il rilevamento esplicito del dispositivo USB su iPhone 14 Pro.</p>
<hr />
<p><em>Basato su esperienza con problemi di routing audio cross-platform e integrazione di periferiche hardware.</em></p>

Q: CodeIgniter active record limit with join A: <p>You did not provide the details of the method call, so we can only make presumptions. If I am using my crystal ball, you are probably calling the model method like <code>getLocations('', '')</code> or <code>getLocations(false, false)</code> - using non-null, yet &quot;empty-ish&quot; values. That would explain the unwanted <code>LIMIT 0</code>.</p>
<p><code>get()</code> and <code>get_where()</code> have a defensive <code>if (!empty($limit))</code> check before calling <code>limit()</code> internally -- this is why your workaround does not experience the data-borne problem.</p>
<p>I recommend type hinting your parameters as well as refining your script to make simpler and fewer query builder calls.</p>
<pre class="lang-php prettyprint-override"><code>public function getLocations(?int $limit = null, ?int $offset = null): array
{
    return $this-&gt;db
        -&gt;join('process', 'process_id')
        -&gt;join('line', 'line_id')
        -&gt;order_by('location_id')
        -&gt;get('location', $limit, $offset)
        -&gt;result();
}
</code></pre>
<hr />
<p>You can verify the query builder model methods' behavior for yourself.</p>
<p><strong>CI's <code>limit()</code> method:</strong> If the limit $value is anything non-null, it is cast as an integer and built in the query builder cache.</p>
<pre class="lang-php prettyprint-override"><code>public function limit($value, $offset = 0)
{
    is_null($value) OR $this-&gt;qb_limit = (int) $value;
    empty($offset) OR $this-&gt;qb_offset = (int) $offset;

    return $this;
}
</code></pre>
<p><strong>CI's <code>get()</code> method:</strong> If $limit is null/emptyish/zeroish, the $limit and $offset parameters will be omitted from the built query cache.</p>
<pre class="lang-php prettyprint-override"><code>public function get($table = '', $limit = NULL, $offset = NULL)
{
    if ($table !== '')
    {
        $this-&gt;_track_aliases($table);
        $this-&gt;from($table);
    }

    if ( ! empty($limit))
    {
        $this-&gt;limit($limit, $offset);
    }

    $result = $this-&gt;query($this-&gt;_compile_select());
    $this-&gt;_reset_select();
    return $result;
}
</code></pre>
<p><strong>CI's <code>get_where()</code> method:</strong> If $limit is null/emptyish/zeroish, the $limit and $offset parameters will be omitted from the built query cache.</p>
<pre class="lang-php prettyprint-override"><code>public function get_where($table = '', $where = NULL, $limit = NULL, $offset = NULL)
{
    if ($table !== '')
    {
        $this-&gt;from($table);
    }

    if ($where !== NULL)
    {
        $this-&gt;where($where);
    }

    if ( ! empty($limit))
    {
        $this-&gt;limit($limit, $offset);
    }

    $result = $this-&gt;query($this-&gt;_compile_select());
    $this-&gt;_reset_select();
    return $result;
}
</code></pre>

Q: ContextMenuStrip and mouse wheel A: <pre><code>    public class MouseWheelContextMenuStrip : System.Windows.Forms.ContextMenuStrip
    {
        private const string ScrollInternalMethodName = &quot;ScrollInternal&quot;;

        private MethodInfo ScrollInternalMethod;
        private MethodInfo UpdateScrollButtonStatusMethod;

        private PropertyInfo DownScrollButtonProperty;
        private PropertyInfo UpScrollButtonProperty;

        public MouseWheelContextMenuStrip() : base() =&gt; this.GetReflectionInfos();
        public MouseWheelContextMenuStrip(System.ComponentModel.IContainer components) : base(components) =&gt; this.GetReflectionInfos();

        protected override void OnMouseWheel(MouseEventArgs e)
        {
            bool up = e.Delta &gt; 0;

            this.UpdateScrollButtonStatusMethod?.Invoke(this, new object[] {});

            bool enabled;

            if (up)
                enabled = this.GetScrollButtonEnabled(this.UpScrollButtonProperty);
            else
                enabled = this.GetScrollButtonEnabled(this.DownScrollButtonProperty);

            if (!enabled) return;

            this.ScrollInternalMethod?.Invoke(this, new object[] { e.Delta &gt; 0 });
        }

        private bool GetScrollButtonEnabled(PropertyInfo pi)
        {
            ToolStripItem btn = pi?.GetValue(this) as ToolStripItem;
            return btn?.Enabled ?? false;
        }

        private void GetReflectionInfos()
        {
            Type t = typeof(ToolStripDropDownMenu);

            this.DownScrollButtonProperty = t.GetProperty(&quot;DownScrollButton&quot;, BindingFlags.NonPublic | BindingFlags.Instance);
            this.UpScrollButtonProperty = t.GetProperty(&quot;UpScrollButton&quot;, BindingFlags.NonPublic | BindingFlags.Instance);
            this.UpdateScrollButtonStatusMethod = t.GetMethod(&quot;UpdateScrollButtonStatus&quot;, BindingFlags.NonPublic | BindingFlags.Instance);

            MethodInfo[] methods = t.GetMethods(BindingFlags.NonPublic | BindingFlags.Instance);

            foreach (MethodInfo method in methods)
            {
                if (method.Name != ScrollInternalMethodName) continue;
                if (method.GetParameters().Length != 1) continue;
                if (method.GetParameters()[0].ParameterType != typeof(bool)) continue;
                this.ScrollInternalMethod = method;
                break;
            }
        }
    }
</code></pre>
<p>I know this is an old question, but this is the solution I came up with that I am happy with.</p>
<p>The WinForms ContextMenuStrip inherits ToolStripDropDownMenu. It has a couple of methods and properties of interest:</p>
<ol>
<li><p>ScrollInternal(bool up)</p>
</li>
<li><p>UpdateScrollButtonStatus()</p>
</li>
<li><p>UpScrollButton property</p>
</li>
<li><p>DownScrollButton property</p>
</li>
</ol>
<p>ScrollInternal calls UpdateScrollButtonStatus but we need to call first to ensure that the Enabled property of the scroll buttons is up-to-date.</p>
<p>We then check the scroll button of the direction we want to scroll to ensure that it is enabled. If it isn't then we should abort scrolling.</p>
<p>The ScrollInternal does check to see if we are at the top item, and won't scroll up past that point, but the down direction lets you scroll past the last visible item and if you scroll to the point where even the last visible item would scroll off the display area, the ScrollInternal method will throw an exception.</p>
<p>Technically you could drop the check for the UpScrollButton enabled state and only check the DownScrollButton enabled state but for symmetry sake I like checking both.</p>
<p>----------</p>
<p>The benefits of this answer is that it doesn't let you scroll past what should be visible and it also doesn't cause problems with the mouse hover like sending the up &amp; down arrow key solutions do.</p>
